Value PORT (\S+)
Value VLAN (\S+)
Value CHANNEL (\S+)

Start
  ^Port\s+Name\s+Status\s+Vlan\s+Duplex\s+Speed\s+Type(?:\s+Flags)?(?:\s+Encapsulation)?\s*$$
  # A routed port carries no VLAN and has no row (FR-009).
  ^\S+(?:\s+\S.*)?\s+(?:connected|notconnect|inactive|errdisabled|disabled)\s+routed\s+\S+\s+\S+\s+(?:Not\s+Present|\S+)(?:\s+\S+)?(?:\s+\S+)?\s*$$
  # A port-channel member (in Po10): its VLAN settings are on the channel's row (feature 008, FR-008).
  # Otherwise a switched port: its access VLAN, or trunk. EOS prints a dot1q-tunnel port like an
  # access port in its outer VLAN (research R3). One rule for both, with a greedy Name, so a
  # description holding a status word ("connected in rack3") is read as the Name, as in
  # show_interfaces_status.textfsm.
  ^${PORT}(?:\s+\S.*)?\s+(?:connected|notconnect|inactive|errdisabled|disabled)\s+(?:in\s+${CHANNEL}|${VLAN})\s+\S+\s+\S+\s+(?:Not\s+Present|\S+)(?:\s+\S+)?(?:\s+\S+)?\s*$$ -> Record
  ^\s*$$
  ^. -> Error

# Same table as show_interfaces_status.textfsm (the interfaces family), read for its Vlan column only.
